n - 1}}\\]for all $n \\ge 1.$  Find $a_{100}.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5657f10c7588dc273038761e00ac1393273447b086b44c2d9ad888523980d086"},"seconds":16.02180027961731,"seed":74299155974357361,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(a_{100}\\) given the recurrence relation \\(a_n = \\frac{a_{n-1}}{1 + a_{n-1}}\\) and the initial condition \\(a_0 = 6\\), we will first investigate the behavior of the sequence by calculating the first few terms and then look for a pattern.\n\nStarting with \\(a_0 = 6\\):\n\n\\[a_1 = \\frac{a_0}{1 + a_0} = \\frac{6}{1 + 6} = \\frac{6}{7}\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(a_2\\):\n\n\\[a_2 = \\frac{a_1}{1 + a_1} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{7}}{1 + \\frac{6}{7}} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{7}}{\\frac{13}{7}} = \\frac{6}{13}\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(a_3\\):\n\n\\[a_3 = \\frac{a_2}{1 + a_2} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{13}}{1 + \\frac{6}{13}} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{13}}{\\frac{19}{13}} = \\frac{6}{19}\\]\n\nFrom these calculations, we observe that the sequence appears to be of the form \\(a_n = \\frac{6}{6n + 1}\\). We will now prove this by induction.\n\n**Base Case:** For \\(n = 0\\), we have \\(a_0 = \\frac{6}{6 \\cdot 0 + 1} = 6\\), which is true.\n\n**Inductive Step:** Assume that \\(a_k = \\frac{6}{6k + 1}\\) for some integer \\(k \\geq 0\\). We need to show that \\(a_{k+1} = \\frac{6}{6(k+1) + 1} = \\frac{6}{6k + 7}\\).\n\nBy the recurrence relation, we have:\n\n\\[a_{k+1} = \\frac{a_k}{1 + a_k} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{6k + 1}}{1 + \\frac{6}{6k + 1}} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{6k + 1}}{\\frac{6k + 1 + 6}{6k + 1}} = \\frac{6}{6k + 7}\\]\n\nThus, the inductive step is proved, and the formula \\(a_n = \\frac{6}{6n + 1}\\) holds for all \\(n \\geq 0\\).\n\nTherefore, for \\(n = 100\\):\n\n\\[a_{100} = \\frac{6}{6 \\cdot 100 + 1} = \\frac{6}{601}\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{6}{601}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{6}{601}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:267"}
